WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.137 --> 00:00:10.181 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Europe episode five of six. First, I clear the chosen names so we
can study a fresh regional group.

2
00:00:10.832 --> 00:00:25.252 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Today we learn nine map targets through the Adriatic and Balkan region, ending
in Greece. Kosovo is included as a GeoClick target; its disputed status will
be explained at its stop.

3
00:00:25.963 --> 00:00:30.270 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Slovenia and its neighbours into a closer view.

4
00:00:37.321 --> 00:00:49.430 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Slovenia sits below Austria, east of Italy and northwest of Croatia. It has a
small Adriatic coastline, despite its largely inland, mountainous outline.

5
00:00:50.087 --> 00:01:04.669 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Postojna Cave is home to the olm, a pale cave-dwelling salamander associated
with stories of baby dragons. Underground wildlife gives Slovenia a memorable
creature of its own.

6
00:01:05.334 --> 00:01:17.060 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep Slovenia beside Italy and Austria, at the head of the Adriatic group.
Slovakia, from our central lesson, is the different country beside Czechia.

7
00:01:20.786 --> 00:01:34.393 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Croatia bends around Bosnia and Herzegovina, with a long Adriatic coast and a
broad inland northern arm. Slovenia is above its western end.

8
00:01:35.049 --> 00:01:46.438 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dubrovnik, on the southern coast, was the city of the maritime Republic of
Ragusa. Its surviving walls connect Croatia’s coastal shape with a history of
sea trade.

9
00:01:47.117 --> 00:01:57.775 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Croatia as the coastal curve around Bosnia, rather than a simple
compact block. Follow its Adriatic edge south toward Montenegro.

10
00:02:01.347 --> 00:02:14.095 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bosnia and Herzegovina sits inside Croatia’s coastal curve, west of Serbia and
north of Montenegro. Its small outlet at Neum reaches the Adriatic.

11
00:02:14.745 --> 00:02:29.803 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Mostar’s Old Bridge was destroyed in 1993 and rebuilt in 2004. The
reconstructed crossing over the Neretva is both an architectural landmark and
a reminder of recovery after war.

12
00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:41.544 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture Bosnia inside Croatia’s bend, with Mostar’s bridge joining two
riverbanks. Do not forget its small stretch of coast between Croatian
sections.

13
00:02:42.624 --> 00:02:46.804 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Montenegro and its neighbours into a closer view.

14
00:02:58.436 --> 00:03:08.502 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Montenegro is the small Adriatic country below Bosnia and Herzegovina and west
of Serbia and Kosovo. Albania is its southern coastal neighbour.

15
00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:22.001 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Montenegro became independent again in 2006 after a referendum ended its union
with Serbia. Its name means black mountain, matching a rugged coastal-country
image.

16
00:03:22.653 --> 00:03:33.485 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the small mountain state between Bosnia and Albania. It has a coast;
Serbia and Kosovo, just inland, do not.

17
00:03:36.195 --> 00:03:50.313 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Serbia is the inland country east of Bosnia and Herzegovina, south of Hungary
and west of Romania and Bulgaria. Its capital, Belgrade, stands where the Sava
meets the Danube.

18
00:03:50.968 --> 00:04:03.426 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The inventor Nikola Tesla was a Serb born in Smiljan, in what is now Croatia.
His museum is in Belgrade: cultural identities and present-day borders can
cross one another.

19
00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:13.843 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Serbia inland beside Bosnia, with the Danube as a northern river
landmark. Keep it separate from the Adriatic countries to its west.

20
00:04:16.922 --> 00:04:27.417 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kosovo is the small landlocked target between Serbia, Montenegro, Albania and
North Macedonia. We include it because GeoClick gives it its own outline.

21
00:04:28.069 --> 00:04:41.978 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kosovo declared independence in 2008. Its status remains disputed, and Serbia
does not recognise that independence; the map target does not mean universal
recognition.

22
00:04:42.636 --> 00:04:56.545 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Use the neighbour pattern to remember it: Albania west, North Macedonia south,
Serbia north and east, Montenegro northwest. Keep the geographic location
distinct from the status dispute.

23
00:04:59.796 --> 00:05:10.373 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Albania is the narrow coastal country below Montenegro, west of North
Macedonia and northwest of Greece. Kosovo lies just beyond its northeastern
edge.

24
00:05:11.023 --> 00:05:24.282 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Under Enver Hoxha’s dictatorship, concrete bunkers were built across Albania.
Some now house museums, turning a landscape of defensive shelters into places
to examine that history.

25
00:05:24.931 --> 00:05:36.657 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Albania as the coastal strip west of North Macedonia. The bunkers are
its historical hook; the Adriatic and Ionian shores give you its western edge.

26
00:05:39.011 --> 00:05:49.332 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Macedonia is the small inland country above Greece and east of Albania.
Kosovo and Serbia lie to its north, with Bulgaria to its east.

27
00:05:49.994 --> 00:06:01.720 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Lake Ohrid, shared with Albania, is an ancient lake with distinctive native
wildlife. Its natural and cultural heritage is recognised by UNESCO.

28
00:06:02.378 --> 00:06:12.525 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep North Macedonia above Greece and away from the sea. Lake Ohrid gives it
water, but a lake shore is not a Mediterranean coastline.

29
00:06:13.238 --> 00:06:17.545 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Greece and its neighbours into a closer view.

30
00:06:20.386 --> 00:06:34.039 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Greece forms the southern end of the Balkan Peninsula, extending into the
Mediterranean with many islands. Albania, North Macedonia and Bulgaria are
above its mainland.

31
00:06:34.697 --> 00:06:47.155 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ancient Athens developed a form of citizen democracy. It was far from
universal voting: women, enslaved people and many residents were excluded from
political rights.

32
00:06:47.813 --> 00:06:59.411 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Greece by its southern peninsula and island-filled seas. Ancient
Athens is the historical hook; the mainland and islands belong to the same
modern country.

33
00:07:05.511 --> 00:07:21.846 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Begin with Slovenia below Austria, then Croatia’s coastal curve around Bosnia
and Herzegovina. Montenegro and Albania continue along the sea; Serbia and
Kosovo sit inland.

34
00:07:22.504 --> 00:07:38.776 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Macedonia lies above Greece and east of Albania. Greece completes the
southern peninsula and its islands. Remember the cave salamander, Dubrovnik,
Mostar’s bridge, and Lake Ohrid as anchors along the route.
